/-! # Completed tame monoid base-change models

Source: ILO, Travaux de Gabber, VI 1.6 and 1.8, printed p. 70;
VIII 3.1.1--3.1.5, pp. 98--99
(blueprint: lem-sfb-completed-tame-monoid-base-change-model).
The general base-change assertion is the agent-formulated algebraic input recorded
in the DAG, rather than a verbatim theorem of ILO. The cokernel may have a free part.
The chart tensor ring, sharpenings, boundary ideals and completed-pair predicate
are the existing definitions recorded by def-per-formal-monoid-extension-data.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v w

namespace Algebra

/-- An injective fs chart map with invertible torsion order in its group cokernel
preserves the two monomial-compatible completed local models. There is no Kummer
or flatness hypothesis. The two implications retain the distinction between the
field model and the Cohen parameter hypersurface model, including their boundary ideals.

Source: ILO VI 1.6 and 1.8, printed p. 70; VIII 3.1.1--3.1.5, pp. 98--99;
agent-formulated algebraic refinement
(blueprint: lem-sfb-completed-tame-monoid-base-change-model).

Proof plan (the fifteen steps of the DAG):
1. The local map $A\to B$ reflects units, so the inverse images $G,F$ of the unit groups are faces
and $h^{-1}F=G$. Apply face sharpening to $(R,G)$ and $(Q,F)$. Put $\Gamma=G^{\rm gp}$ and
$\Delta=F^{\rm gp}$. It gives $R_G^\times=\Gamma$, $Q_F^\times=\Delta$, and sharp fs $S,T$ with
finite free group completions. Apply the group-section monoid-product lemma to each quotient: its
kernel condition follows from $S^{\rm gp}=R^{\rm gp}/\Gamma$ and $T^{\rm gp}=Q^{\rm gp}/\Delta$.
Choose sections and write $R_G=\Gamma\oplus S$, $Q_F=\Delta\oplus T$. Then
$$h(g,s)=(h_U(g)+c(s),\beta(s)),\quad \alpha(g,s)=\rho(g)e(s)a(s),$$ where $h_U$ is injective,
$c:S^{\rm gp}\to\Delta$, $\rho=\alpha|_\Gamma$, and $e(s)=\epsilon(j_S(s))$ are characters. In
these coordinates $a(s)$ uses the given identification with $S$. If $\beta(s)=0$, a representative
of $s$ maps to a unit of $Q_F$ and hence to a unit of $B$; its image in $A$ is a unit, so $s=0$.
Thus $\beta^{-1}(0)=\{0\}$. No injectivity of $\beta^{\rm gp}$ is asserted. Uses:
lem-trf-fs-face-sharpening, lem-trf-group-section-monoid-product.
2. Set $N=\#(\operatorname{coker}h^{\rm gp})_{\rm tor}$, a positive unit in $A$. The torsion
subgroup of $\Delta/h_U(\Gamma)$ injects into $(\operatorname{coker}h^{\rm gp})_{\rm tor}$: if
$dv=h_U(g)$, and $v=h^{\rm gp}(r)$, injectivity gives $dr=g\in\Gamma$; since $R^{\rm
gp}/\Gamma=S^{\rm gp}$ is torsion free, $r\in\Gamma$ and $v\in h_U(\Gamma)$. This proves
injectivity on torsion, by applying the same argument to the difference of two such classes.
Consequently this torsion subgroup is finite and killed by $N$. The group $\Delta$ is finitely
generated: split $Q^{\rm gp}\to T^{\rm gp}$ by lifting its finite basis and project the finite
generators of $Q^{\rm gp}$ to the kernel $\Delta$. The same argument gives finite generation of
$\Gamma$. Let $\Gamma'\subset\Delta$ be the inverse image of that torsion subgroup. Then
$\Gamma'/h_U(\Gamma)$ is finite killed by $N$, so $\Gamma'$ is generated by generators of
$h_U(\Gamma)$ and finitely many coset representatives. The quotient $\Delta/\Gamma'$ is finitely
generated and torsion free, hence has a finite basis by the cited group theorem. Lift that basis
to split the sequence, obtaining $\Delta=\Gamma'\oplus\mathbf Z^r$. In particular the full unit
quotient is allowed to have a free part. Uses: lem-trf-fs-face-sharpening,
lem-trf-fg-torsionfree-group-free.
3. We may replace $A$ by $A_0=\widehat A$ without changing $\widehat B$, its residue field or its
chart monomials. Indeed let $C'=\widehat A\otimes_A C$. Since $\widehat A/\mathfrak m\widehat
A=A/\mathfrak m$, there is a unique prime over the selected point in the closed fibre. Apply the
finite-type base-change adic-quotient lemma to $A\to C$: $C$ is finite type because $Q$ has
finitely many monoid generators. That lemma identifies every quotient of the two selected local
rings by its maximal-ideal power, compatibly with transitions. Taking inverse limits gives the
asserted identification, preserving each original monomial and hence its generated ideal.
Finite-type permanence and localization permanence make all these local rings Noetherian. Uses:
lem-trf-finite-type-base-change-adic-quotients, lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra-noetherian,
lem-weil-pbc-localization-noetherian.
4. Write $L=k$ in (a) and $L=V$ in (b), and put $E=L[[t_1,\ldots,t_n]]$. The power-series theorem
gives a nonzero complete regular Noetherian local $E$. The defining combined-adic completions
identify the supplied base model with $$A_0=E[[S]]/(f),\qquad f=0\text{ in (a)},\quad
f=\theta\text{ in (b)}.$$ For clarity, modulo the $j$th power of the combined ideal only
coefficients modulo $(\mathfrak m_L,t)^j$ are needed. The completion-mod-power identity identifies
these with coefficients of $E$ modulo $\mathfrak m_E^j$, so the two quotient rings and their
transitions coincide. In case (b) $f$ lies in the maximal ideal, since $\theta\equiv p$ modulo the
boundary and $t$; in case (a) it is zero. The formal monoid dimension theorem also gives
$E[[S]]/([s]:s\ne0)=E$. Uses: lem-weil-pbc-power-series-field-cohen-regular-complete,
lem-weil-pbc-adic-completion-mod-power, lem-trf-formal-monoid-ring-dimension.
5. Localize the tensor ring by all $F$-monomials, which are already invertible in $B$. This gives
$A_0\otimes_{\mathbf Z[R_G]}\mathbf Z[Q_F]$: its maps send $b\otimes z^{q-f}$ to $bz^q/z^f$ and
conversely, and balancing accounts for the already inverted $G$-monomials. With the sections of
Step 1 put $$Z=A_0\otimes_{\mathbf Z[\Gamma]}\mathbf Z[\Delta].$$ Then the localized chart algebra
is $$Z[T]/\bigl([c(s)]Y^{\beta(s)}-e(s)a(s):s\in S\bigr).$$ These are actual inverse
presentations: the map on $Q_F=\Delta\oplus T$ sends $(v,t)$ to $[v]Y^t$, and the inverse sends
$[v]$ and $Y^t$ to the corresponding $Q_F$-monomials. The relations for $(g,s)\in\Gamma\oplus S$
are generated by those for $(g,0)$ and $(0,s)$, giving precisely the displayed relations by the
tensor pushout universal property. A finite set of monoid generators of $S$ suffices, since the
relation for $s+s'$ is the sum of a monomial multiple of the relation for $s$ and another multiple
of the relation for $s'$. Uses: lem-weil-pbc-algebra-tensor-pushout-universal-property,
def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime.
6. The decomposition $\Delta=\Gamma'\oplus\mathbf Z^r$ of Step 2 identifies
$$Z=A_0[z_1^{\pm1},\ldots,z_r^{\pm1}]\otimes_{\mathbf Z[\Gamma]}\mathbf Z[\Gamma'].$$ The
finite-index group-algebra theorem applies to $h_U:\Gamma\hookrightarrow\Gamma'$ and the character
$\rho$, with exponent $N$. It makes $Z$ finite free and etale over $P=A_0[z^{\pm1}]$. Let
$\mathfrak a$ be the inverse image in $Z$ of the maximal ideal of $B$. It contracts to the maximal
ideal of $A_0$. The inverse-image prime of the selected point in $Z[T]$ is exactly $\mathfrak
aZ[T]+(Y^t:t\ne0)$: every such monomial vanishes in its residue field, and quotienting by them
leaves $Z$. Its residue field is $\kappa(\mathfrak a)=\kappa(\mathfrak q)$, since localization of
this quotient at the selected prime leaves $\operatorname{Frac}(Z/\mathfrak a)$. Write $\mathfrak
b=\mathfrak a\cap P$. Uses: lem-sfb-tame-kummer-group-algebra-completion.
7. Let $H=E[[S]]$ and $U=E[z^{\pm1}]$. Since $P=H[z^{\pm1}]/(f)$ and the selected prime contains
the maximal ideal of $A_0$, it contains the images of all nonzero $S$-monomials. Modulo those
monomials, $f$ and $\mathfrak m_E$ vanish at the selected point. There is therefore a unique prime
$\mathfrak p\subset U$ containing $\mathfrak m_EU$ such that the inverse image of $\mathfrak b$ in
$H[z^{\pm1}]$ is $\mathfrak pH[z^{\pm1}]+([s]:s\ne0)$. Set $D_0=\widehat{U_{\mathfrak p}}$. Apply
the Laurent--monoid completion comparison with its second monoid trivial. Completion of the
quotient by $f$ then gives $$\widehat{P_{\mathfrak b}}\cong D_0[[S]]/(f_0),$$ where $f_0$ is the
coefficientwise image of $f$. Here $D_0$ has residue field $\kappa(\mathfrak b)$; the prime
classification follows from $H/([s]:s\ne0)=E$, as in Step 4. Uses:
lem-sfb-laurent-monoid-completion, lem-weil-pbc-local-completion-quotient.
8. The ring $D_0$ is nonzero complete regular Noetherian local. Indeed $E$ is regular local by
Step 4, hence a regular ring by regular-local localization; polynomial regularity makes
$E[z_1,\ldots,z_r]$ regular. Its prime local ring at the prime corresponding to $\mathfrak p$ is
$U_{\mathfrak p}$ (the variables are units there), so that ring is regular local. Its completion
is regular local, Noetherian and complete by the cited completion theorems, and has the same
nonzero residue field. These arguments construct $D_0$ before the relation $f_0$ is imposed. Uses:
lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-localization, lem-trf-polynomial-ring-regular-base,
lem-weil-pbc-completion-regular-local, lem-weil-pbc-adic-completion-noetherian,
lem-weil-pbc-adic-completion-mod-ideal, lem-weil-pbc-local-completion-local-ring.
9. Apply finite-extension completion to the finite $P$-algebra $Z$ at $\mathfrak b$. It identifies
$\widehat{Z_{\mathfrak a}}$ with the selected local factor of $\widehat{P_{\mathfrak b}}\otimes_P
Z$. Equivalently, apply the tame group-algebra theorem after replacing its coefficient ring by the
complete local ring $B_0=D_0[[S]]/(f_0)$: it decomposes the same algebra into complete local
finite etale $B_0$-algebras. The selected factor $K$ is nonzero and has residue field
$\kappa(\mathfrak a)=\kappa(\mathfrak q)$. Apply finite-etale formal-monoid coefficient lifting
with coefficient ring $D_0$, monoid $S$, and relation $f_0$ (zero is permitted). We obtain a
nonzero complete regular Noetherian local finite etale $D_0$-algebra $D$ and a $B_0$-algebra
isomorphism $$K\cong D[[S]]/(f_D).$$ The relation $f_D$ is the coefficientwise image of the
original $f$. The lemma also states that each nonzero cotangent class from $D_0$ remains nonzero
in $D$. Uses: lem-weil-pbc-completion-finite-extension,
lem-sfb-tame-kummer-group-algebra-completion, lem-sfb-finite-etale-formal-monoid-coefficient-lift.
10. Completing the presentation in Step 5 at its selected prime first replaces $Z_{\mathfrak a}$
by $K=\widehat{Z_{\mathfrak a}}$. To check this explicitly, reduction of coefficients modulo the
$j$th maximal-ideal power gives the same ring by completion-mod-power; all $T$-monomials have
positive order, and denominators outside the selected prime have nonzero constant coefficient
modulo $\mathfrak a$, hence are invertible modulo that power by a finite geometric series. These
identifications preserve the finitely many relations of Step 5. Completion of a Noetherian
quotient therefore gives the completed presentation over $K$ with those relations. Every ring
before completion is finite type over a Noetherian coefficient ring, including Laurent rings
(adjoin inverses), so the quotient-completion hypotheses hold; their localizations and completions
are Noetherian by the cited theorems. Uses: lem-weil-pbc-adic-completion-mod-power,
lem-weil-pbc-local-completion-quotient, lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra-noetherian,
lem-weil-pbc-localization-noetherian, lem-weil-pbc-adic-completion-noetherian.
11. In $K$, the ratios $e(s)[c(s)]^{-1}$ form a character of the free group $S^{\rm gp}$. Choose
unit lifts of its values on a finite basis to $D[[S]]$ and extend multiplicatively to obtain a
character $v:S^{\rm gp}\to D[[S]]^\times$. Unit lifts exist because the kernel $(f_D)$ is
contained in the maximal ideal: an element whose quotient is a unit has nonzero residue, hence is
a unit. Under $K=D[[S]]/(f_D)$, the remaining equations are $$Y^{\beta(s)}-v(s)X^s=0.$$ The new
completion-comparison lemma, now with coefficient ring $D$, no Laurent variables, and monoids
$S,T$, identifies the completed ring before these equations and $f_D$ with $D[[S\oplus T]]$.
Completion of the Noetherian quotient thus proves $$\widehat B\cong D[[S\oplus T]]/(f_D,\
X^s-v(s)^{-1}Y^{\beta(s)}:s\in S).$$ Changing the chosen lifts modifies each equation by an
element of $(f_D)$, so the displayed quotient is independent of that choice. This presentation
retains the original character; it never extends $\epsilon$ across $h^{\rm gp}$. Uses:
lem-trf-fs-face-sharpening, lem-sfb-laurent-monoid-completion,
lem-weil-pbc-local-completion-quotient.
12. Apply formal-monoid implicit elimination with coefficient ring $D$, monoids $S,T$, local
monoid map $\beta$ of Step 1 and character $u(s)=v(s)^{-1}$. Its statement identifies the quotient
by $X^s-u(s)Y^{\beta(s)}$ with $D[[T]]$, fixes $D$ on the stratum, and sends every nonzero
$S$-monomial into the nonzero-$T$ ideal. Thus Step 11 yields $\widehat B\cong D[[T]]$ when $f=0$,
and $\widehat B\cong D[[T]]/(\eta)$ when $f=\theta$, with $\eta$ the full convergent substitution
image of $f_D$. This uses the entire substituted series, including its coefficient variables,
rather than its constant coefficient. Uses: lem-sfb-formal-monoid-implicit-elimination.
13. It remains to check the parameter condition in (b). Put $R_*=U_{\mathfrak
p}/(t_1,\ldots,t_n)$. It is the localization of $V[z^{\pm1}]$ at the induced prime, and $R_*/pR_*$
is the corresponding prime localization of $k[z^{\pm1}]$. Both are regular local by regular-local
localization and polynomial regularity over $V$ and $k$; those coefficient rings are regular by
the power-series theorem with zero variables. Also $p\ne0$ in $R_*$, since multiplication by $p$
is injective on the Laurent polynomial ring over the domain $V$ and remains injective after
localization. The nonzero proper ideal $pR_*$ has regular quotient. The regular-quotient parameter
theorem makes it generated by a nonempty part of a minimal parameter system. If $p$ belonged to
$\mathfrak m_{R_*}^2$, every generator of $pR_*$ would have zero cotangent class, contradicting
minimality (equivalently the regular-local cotangent-dimension theorem). Hence $p\in\mathfrak
m_{R_*}\setminus\mathfrak m_{R_*}^2$. Uses:
lem-weil-pbc-power-series-field-cohen-regular-complete, lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-localization,
lem-trf-polynomial-ring-regular-base, lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-quotient-kernel-parameters,
lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-cotangent-dimension.
14. By completion of the quotient, $D_0/(t)D_0\cong\widehat{R_*}$. Completion-mod-power at power
two preserves the cotangent class of $p$, so it is nonzero in $\widehat{R_*}$. Let $\theta_{00}\in
D_0$ be the image of $\theta$ after setting all nonzero $S$-monomials to zero and extending
coefficients from $E$. The hypothesis gives $\theta_{00}\equiv p\pmod{(t)D_0}$. Therefore
$\theta_{00}\in\mathfrak m_{D_0}\setminus\mathfrak m_{D_0}^2$: its residue vanishes, and a square
class would have square image in $D_0/(t)D_0$, contrary to Step 13. Coefficient lifting in Step 9
preserves this nonzero cotangent class in $D$. Finally implicit elimination fixes coefficients on
the stratum and kills nonzero $S$-monomials there, so $\overline\eta$ is exactly the image of
$\theta_{00}$ in $D$. This proves $\overline\eta\in\mathfrak m_D\setminus\mathfrak m_D^2$,
including linear terms such as $\theta=p-t_1$. Uses: lem-weil-pbc-local-completion-quotient,
lem-weil-pbc-adic-completion-mod-power, lem-sfb-finite-etale-formal-monoid-coefficient-lift,
lem-sfb-formal-monoid-implicit-elimination.
15. The isomorphisms identify the indicated ideals. A monomial $q-f\in Q_F=\Delta\oplus T$ with
$T$-class $t$ maps to a unit from $K$ times $Y^t$; after Step 12 it is still a unit times $Y^t$.
For $q\in Q\setminus F$ its class is nonzero, giving one ideal inclusion. Conversely each $t\ne0$
has a representative $q-f$ with $q\in Q$, $f\in F$ and necessarily $q\notin F$. The denominator
monomial and its $\Delta$-factor are units. Hence $Y^t$ is a unit times an image of a generator of
$J$. This gives the reverse inclusion as generated ideals, including after the quotient by $\eta$.
Every isomorphism identifies maximal ideals and therefore their powers, so it and its inverse are
continuous. The residue field of $D$ is $\kappa(\mathfrak q)$ by Step 9. If $T=\{0\}$ the two
boundary ideals are zero; if $r=0$ or $n=0$ the empty variable lists give the same constructions.
Uses: lem-sfb-formal-monoid-implicit-elimination.
-/
theorem completedTameMonoidBaseChange_model
    (A : Type u) [CommRing A] [IsNoetherianRing A] [IsLocalRing A]
    (R : Type v) [CommMonoid R] (Q : Type w) [CommMonoid Q]
    (_hR : AlgebraicGeometry.IsFineSaturatedMonoid R)
    (_hQ : AlgebraicGeometry.IsFineSaturatedMonoid Q) (h : R →* Q)
    (_hgp : Function.Injective
      (GrothendieckGroup.lift ((GrothendieckGroup.of (M := Q)).comp h)))
    (_htor : Finite (CommGroup.torsion
      (GrothendieckGroup Q ⧸
        (GrothendieckGroup.lift ((GrothendieckGroup.of (M := Q)).comp h)).range)))
    (_horder : IsUnit (Nat.card (CommGroup.torsion
      (GrothendieckGroup Q ⧸
        (GrothendieckGroup.lift ((GrothendieckGroup.of (M := Q)).comp h)).range)) : A))
    (α : R →* A) (a : Multiplicative (chartSharpMonoid α) →* A)
    (ε : GrothendieckGroup R →* Aˣ)
    (_hα : ∀ r : Localization ((IsUnit.submonoid A).comap α),
      localizedUnitChart α r =
        (ε (unitChartGroupMap α r) : A) * a (Multiplicative.ofAdd
          (Additive.ofMul (Associates.mk r))))
    (q : Ideal (monoidChartTensor α h)) [q.IsPrime]
    (_hq : Ideal.comap (monoidChartTensorBase α h) q = IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A) :
    ((∃ (n : ℕ)
        (e : AddMonoidAlgebra.CompletedLocalModel (IsLocalRing.ResidueField A)
          (chartSharpMonoid α) n ≃+* AdicCompletion (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A) A),
        ∀ s : chartSharpMonoid α,
          e (algebraMap (MvPolynomial (Fin n)
              (AddMonoidAlgebra (IsLocalRing.ResidueField A) (chartSharpMonoid α))) _
            (MvPolynomial.C (AddMonoidAlgebra.single s (1 : IsLocalRing.ResidueField A)))) =
            algebraMap A _ (a (Multiplicative.ofAdd s))) →
      AddMonoidAlgebra.HasExtendedFormalMonoidModel (Localization.AtPrime q)
        (chartSharpMonoid (localizedTensorMonomial α h q))
        (localizedTensorBoundary α h q) false) ∧
    ((∃ (V : Type (max u v w)) (_ : CommRing V) (_ : IsLocalRing V) (p : ℕ),
        IsCohenRing p V ∧
        Nonempty (IsLocalRing.ResidueField V ≃+* IsLocalRing.ResidueField A) ∧
        ∃ (n : ℕ) (θ : AddMonoidAlgebra.CompletedLocalModel V (chartSharpMonoid α) n),
          θ - (p : AddMonoidAlgebra.CompletedLocalModel V (chartSharpMonoid α) n) ∈
            AddMonoidAlgebra.completedLocalModelBoundaryIdeal V (chartSharpMonoid α) n ∧
          ∃ e : (AddMonoidAlgebra.CompletedLocalModel V (chartSharpMonoid α) n ⧸
              Ideal.span {θ}) ≃+* AdicCompletion (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A) A,
            ∀ s : chartSharpMonoid α,
              e (Ideal.Quotient.mk (Ideal.span {θ})
                (algebraMap (MvPolynomial (Fin n)
                    (AddMonoidAlgebra V (chartSharpMonoid α))) _
                  (MvPolynomial.C (AddMonoidAlgebra.single s (1 : V))))) =
                  algebraMap A _ (a (Multiplicative.ofAdd s))) →
      AddMonoidAlgebra.HasExtendedFormalMonoidModel (Localization.AtPrime q)
        (chartSharpMonoid (localizedTensorMonomial α h q))
        (localizedTensorBoundary α h q) true) := by
  sorry


end Algebra
